================================================================================
3. PER-KEY JUDGEMENT (hi)
================================================================================
(1) ui.chat.composer_media.pasted_image = 'पेस्ट की गई तस्वीर'   ["Pasted image"]
Fallback file name of an image pasted with no usable name, also a short caption; no extension.
- Meaning: exact. पेस्ट की गई = "was pasted" (perfect participle, feminine) + तस्वीर = "image".
- Internal precedent: the sibling key renders identically in shape —
  ui.chat.composer_media.pasted_file = 'पेस्ट की गई फ़ाइल'; की गई agrees with its feminine head
  (फ़ाइल / तस्वीर) in both. pasted_type = 'पेस्ट किया गया {mimeType}' keeps the masculine form
  because its head is the MIME token. So the three agree with each other exactly as they should.
- Term: तस्वीर is this catalogue's established word for "image" — ui.chat.canonical.image_label
  = 'तस्वीर', and every ui.chat.image_actions.* sibling uses तस्वीर ('तस्वीर कॉपी करें',
  'तस्वीर बड़ी करें'). छवि/चित्र exist only in older avatar-editor error prose; तस्वीर is the
  user-facing term and the right one here.
- Context note compliance: no extension, no MIME identifier, no placeholder — none present.
  Compact enough for the narrow caption (18 codepoints, and it is the same length class as the
  existing pasted_file value that already ships that tile).
- Style guide: no nukta missing, no danda (English has no full stop), no तुम/तू, no padding.

(2) ui.common.actions.actions_for_name = '{name} की कार्रवाइयाँ'   ["Actions for {name}"]
aria-label/tooltip of a file's actions menu; {name} is the user's own file name, verbatim.
- Placeholder: {name} byte-identical, plain, uninflected; no ICU needed. The suffix is a fixed
  postpositional phrase: की agrees with कार्रवाइयाँ (feminine plural), never with the inserted
  string, so no case/gender/number of a user name can break it. Proven by stress.py on ten
  adversarial insertions, including a name that already ends in a Hindi genitive phrase.
- Byte-identical precedent for the SAME English string in this catalogue:
  ui.chat.canonical.link_toolkit.actions_for = '{name} की कार्रवाइयाँ'  (en side: 'Actions for {name}').
  Also ui.shell.sidebar_navigation.row_actions and ui.projects.project_board.actions_for_displayname
  use the same construction. (The catalogue also contains '{name} के लिए कार्रवाइयाँ' at
  ui.library.menu.asset_actions_label and ui.settings.appearance.editor.more — both renderings are
  live for "actions for X". Reusing the exact bytes of the identical-English precedent is the
  stronger choice, and that is what shipped.)
- Meaning: "X's actions" identifies the asset whose menu opens, which is what the context note
  asks for ("Identifies the asset whose actions will open; not a command to act on the name").
- Register: neutral noun phrase, no honorifics, no imperative — correct for an aria-label.

(3) ui.chat.image_actions.actions = 'तस्वीर की कार्रवाइयाँ'   ["Image actions"]
Default tooltip and unnamed-image trigger accessible name; a named image uses key (2).
- Byte-identical precedent: ui.canvas.files.imageActions = 'तस्वीर की कार्रवाइयाँ' whose English
  is also exactly 'Image actions'. Same family form as ui.teamBridge.actions.label =
  'संदेश की कार्रवाइयाँ' and ui.chat.header.actions = 'बातचीत की कार्रवाइयाँ'.
- Meaning and role: short noun phrase, sentence case, no trailing punctuation — matches the
  context note and does not collide with the generic file wording or key (2).

(4) ui.chat.audio.position_label = '{label} की प्लेबैक स्थिति'   ["{label} position"]
Accessible name of an audio clip's seek slider; {label} is the clip's display title, verbatim.
- Placeholder: {label} byte-identical, plain; unchanged suffix for any inserted title
  (stress.py). Two clips may be present, and the label is retained, as the note requires.
- Term: the catalogue's own standalone key for "Playback position" is
  ui.chat.attachments.playback_position = 'प्लेबैक की स्थिति' (and playback_rate =
  'प्लेबैक की गति'). This value composes the same two words as a compound, 'प्लेबैक स्थिति',
  because the genitive is already carried by the preceding '{label} की' — writing की twice
  ("{label} की प्लेबैक की स्थिति") is the stilted form. The glossary's own rule settles that
  this is not a term divergence: "A row is violated by a different *term*, not by a grammar
  form." प्लेबैक and स्थिति are unchanged; only the linking particle moves.
- Why प्लेबैक is present although the English says only "position": स्थिति alone is this
  catalogue's word for *status* (~19 existing strings: 'कनेक्शन की स्थिति', 'पढ़ने की स्थिति',
  'फ़ाइल की स्थिति'…). "X की स्थिति" on a media slider would read as "X's status". The
  disambiguator is required by the target language and matches the note's wording ("Names
  playback position for this specific clip"). Recorded here as considered and cleared, not a
  finding.
- Avoids the play/pause collision: the sibling controls keep their verb construction
  ('{label} चलाएँ' / '{label} रोकें'), so the position slider is distinct from both.

